# Route-parity probe — the three install routes serve THE SAME product
#
#   npm run release:parity | tee deepblend/docs/probe-route-parity.log
#
# Taken 2026-09-23 (commercial-readiness ledger C2). What it is evidence for: `CONTRIBUTING.md` §5's
# three install routes and `install.md` §1's claim that they are three ways to the same product.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Every route had been verified on its own — the three `probe-dsh-plugin-*.log` files
# are three separate readings taken with the same criteria — and NOTHING asserted that they agree.
# "Three commands run three times" is a habit, not a check, and the three fetch from three different
# places (the default branch, the registry, the Release asset), so they can disagree. The way they
# disagree is quiet: each one reports success about a different product.
#
# HOW TO READ IT. Each route is installed once into its own scratch `$DSH_HOME` and read back. The
# comparison at the end is the point:
#
#   installed version   identical across the three, AND equal to `deepblend/version.json`. The
#                       repository's own statement is the third opinion: two stale routes agreeing
#                       with each other is a state this check would otherwise call healthy.
#   workbench route     `GET /deepblend/workbench` answers HTTP 200 on all three. A version number is
#                       what an artifact says about itself; this route is what only the CURRENT
#                       version has, so a stale artifact answers 404 while honestly claiming an old
#                       version.
#   presets deployed    the same ids, byte-identical to `deepblend/presets/`.
#   packages fetched    AND ONE READING THAT MUST DIFFER: 7 / 7 / 1. The tarball carries its siblings
#                       inside the artifact and the other two resolve them. Asserting the difference is
#                       what stops "they agree" from meaning "nothing was measured".
#
# The pnpm store is EMPTY for this run. The tarball's URL is version-free by design (the market's
# `latest/download/` rule), so it is byte-identical between releases and pnpm's store — which is keyed
# by that URL — would hand back the previous artifact on a machine that had installed it.
#
# IT CAN FAIL, AND HERE IS HOW THAT WAS SHOWN. One route's spec can be overridden to point at an older
# published version, and the check must report the disagreement:
#
#   DEEPBLEND_PARITY_NPM_SPEC='@deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle@0.2.1' npm run release:parity
#   → PROBLEM: the three routes serve 2 different versions:
#       @deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle@0.2.2 | @deepblend/dsh-blender-bundle@0.2.1
#   → problems: 2, exit 1
#
# A comparison that has never been seen to report a disagreement is a comparison nobody has tested.


